w11 for Fedora
======================

Nothing is published anywhere yet -- COPR and Fedora proper are the owner's
call, not a licence problem any more: the tree is BSD-2-Clause since HEAD
375d815, which is the valid SPDX expression Fedora requires, and the spec says
`License: BSD-2-Clause` with `%license LICENSE` in %files.  Today the route is:
clone, build, install your own build.

  sh scripts/build-rpm.sh            # -> dist/*.rpm, three noarch packages
  sudo dnf install ./dist/*.rpm

`--no-deps` fails instead of installing build tools, `--lint` runs rpmlint
with packaging/rpm/w11.rpmlintrc, `--keep` leaves the scratch topdir
under dist/rpmbuild, and `--help` prints the header of the script.

Three packages, not one
-----------------------

  w11
        the six tools -- seven commands, counting xw11 -- the udev rule, the
        modules-load line and the warandr menu entry.
  gnome-shell-extension-w11-bridge
        the bridge extension, the per-user enabler and its autostart entry.
        `Supplements: (w11 and gnome-shell)`, so dnf installs it by
        itself on a machine that has both halves and on no other -- a sway or
        KDE box gets the tools and no extension.  The .deb cannot say that;
        it carries the extension everywhere.
  gnome-shell-extension-w11-overlap
        the second, separate extension, the one behind
        `wxrandr --unsafe-gnome-overlap`.  No Supplements, on purpose:
        nothing installs it for you, nothing enables it, and it is the only
        thing here that can cost you the session you are sitting in.

What differs from the .deb, and why
-----------------------------------

  * Python lands in %{python3_sitelib} = /usr/lib/python3.14/site-packages,
    which is version-bound, so the auto-generated dependency is
    `python(abi) = 3.14`.  Fedora 43 and 44 both ship python3 3.14.7, so one
    noarch build covers both; rawhide is 3.15 and needs its own.  (The .deb's
    "one file for both releases" story survives here only by accident of two
    releases sharing a python.)
  * The helper is /usr/libexec/w11/enable-bridge.  It is the same
    script the .deb installs -- packaging/common/enable-bridge -- and only the
    autostart entry's Exec= line is rewritten at build time.
  * /etc/xdg/autostart/w11-enable-bridge.desktop is a plain file, not
    a symlink.  rpm marks nothing %config unless the spec says so, so an erase
    takes it; debhelper would have made it a conffile that outlives the
    package, which is the reason for the .deb's symlink.
  * GTK is `Recommends: python3-gobject gtk3`, not a hard dependency.  Weak
    deps are on by default in dnf, so the warandr GUI still arrives for
    everyone who has not turned them off, and `dnf install w11` on a
    box with no GNOME pulls in no toolkit.  Fedora has no gir1.2-* split:
    Gtk-3.0.typelib is inside gtk3 itself.
  * The X11 handover's install hint names four packages, not two: Fedora ships
    one binary per package (xprop, xrandr), where Debian has x11-utils and
    x11-xserver-utils.  The tools print the right one for the machine they are
    on -- see w11common/distro.py.
  * `acl` is not on a Fedora cloud image (measured: no setfacl, no getfacl),
    so the removal's ACL teardown takes the python fallback
    (`os.removexattr`).  Nothing here requires acl for it.

/dev/uinput
-----------

Fedora's own 70-uaccess.rules does not mention uinput (measured on Fedora 44),
so the node is root:root 0600 with no ACL until this package's rule tags it
`uaccess`.  %post applies the rule to the running system, so no reboot is
needed; %postun on the last erase puts the node back.  The reload in %post is
ours on purpose although systemd-udev carries a file trigger on
/usr/lib/udev/rules.d: that trigger runs at the end of the transaction, after
this scriptlet, so without our own reload the `udevadm trigger` that follows
would re-tag the node against the old rule set.

Anyone who can open /dev/uinput can type as you.  The threat model is in
README.md.

SELinux
-------

Nothing in the payload asks for a non-default label: the commands are
/usr/bin scripts (bin_t), the extensions are /usr/share data (usr_t), the
helper is under /usr/libexec.  A desktop user on Fedora is unconfined, so
opening /dev/uinput once the ACL is there needs no policy of ours.  The rig's
fedora44-* flavors assert that `ausearch -m avc` is empty after the tools have
run; until those goldens are built, that claim is untested rather than proven.

The bridge, and the one re-login
--------------------------------

Identical to the .deb, down to the stamp: gnome-shell scans extension
directories only at login, enabling an extension is a per-user dconf setting,
and rpm runs as root with possibly no session on the machine.  So the
extension is installed system-wide and enabled in each user's first GNOME
session by the autostart entry.  Install, log out, log back in.

